WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:04.879
Okay, welcome to theory and practice for ICV effects.

2
00:00:04.879 --> 00:00:10.199
So before the advent of ICV effects, the workflow for most visual effect centric films

3
00:00:10.199 --> 00:00:14.900
followed a fairly classical approach to producing the final image.

4
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.839
A distinct production phases were followed that included development, pre-production,

5
00:00:19.839 --> 00:00:23.620
production, post-production, and distribution.

6
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:29.640
The classical workflow placed the production emphasis on two primary phases, those being

7
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.359
production and post-production.

8
00:00:32.359 --> 00:00:37.460
Filmmakers and producers emphasized the adage of, well, let's just fix it in post.

9
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:43.000
This helped to address various animation and visual effects issues and problems and work

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.679
while also addressing various problems that may have been encountered on stage.

11
00:00:49.679 --> 00:00:54.799
So anything that needed to be modified in principle photography, they fixed it in

12
00:00:54.799 --> 00:00:55.799
post-production.

13
00:00:56.079 --> 00:01:03.679
Now, while pre-visualization during pre-production had its benefit in helping various producers

14
00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:10.909
and directors be able to visualize what was needed to be shot during production, it was

15
00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:11.909
a slower process.

16
00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:19.769
It was highly team-based, key-framed, animated, and using traditional computer graphics.

17
00:01:19.769 --> 00:01:22.530
No real-time capabilities.

18
00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:26.569
So everything needed to be playblasted or rendered and composited.

19
00:01:26.569 --> 00:01:32.109
And basically, this whole process, especially with computer graphics, had a tendency to

20
00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:36.409
bifurcate or split the whole creative process.

21
00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:42.209
The director didn't really have the opportunity very well to see what was going on in these

22
00:01:42.209 --> 00:01:44.849
various post-production phases.

23
00:01:44.849 --> 00:01:49.890
Shots could potentially take days or weeks in order to see something out of it.

24
00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.930
And even pre-visualization, though it was faster, it was not a very good quality.

25
00:01:54.930 --> 00:02:01.769
And it was rather low resolution and, at best, provided at least some basic information.

26
00:02:01.769 --> 00:02:06.870
If we look at what the ICV effects production workflow looks like for live action, it's

27
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.669
a little bit different.

28
00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:13.530
So there's still the whole process of development where you have storyboards and scripts and

29
00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:18.469
you're basically trying to come up with your basic idea.

30
00:02:18.469 --> 00:02:24.210
But when moved into pre-production, instead of just simply going into a previs phase and

31
00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:29.229
then into an eventual production phase where you're shooting on stage, the pre-production

32
00:02:29.229 --> 00:02:33.509
element in ICV effects is a little more involved.

33
00:02:33.509 --> 00:02:37.990
You do still have an art department, but the art department is supplemented with a

34
00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.389
VAD or a virtual art department.

35
00:02:40.389 --> 00:02:47.629
And this virtual art department basically spends its time perfecting a lot of the designs

36
00:02:47.669 --> 00:02:52.229
and 3D graphics that are being generated by the art department in order for it to be

37
00:02:52.229 --> 00:02:57.069
used in a live action and real-time environment.

38
00:02:57.069 --> 00:03:05.150
So you get this cycle of the art department working with the VAD, who is also then talking

39
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:11.229
to the previs team, and we also have the visual effects team that's a part of the

40
00:03:11.229 --> 00:03:13.189
pre-production process now.

41
00:03:13.189 --> 00:03:16.270
So it's not that they weren't involved previously.

42
00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:22.669
It's just now in ICV effects, they become an essential factor with these other three

43
00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:28.550
elements in order to prepare content to go onto the actual stage.

44
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:34.469
So when we move onto the stage in production itself and we have an LED volume, this is

45
00:03:34.469 --> 00:03:39.430
where things are actually being shot and it's being shot in camera in combination

46
00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:45.810
with all of your foreground, mid-ground, and background elements, and it's all being done

47
00:03:45.849 --> 00:03:46.849
in real-time.

48
00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:49.689
That's why we call it in-camera visual effects.

49
00:03:49.689 --> 00:03:54.889
All of these shots then that are shot and captured are sent over to editorial, where

50
00:03:54.889 --> 00:04:00.449
editorial continues the process of putting it into a sequence, and then it is then

51
00:04:00.449 --> 00:04:06.810
delivered to the post-production facility where VFX can be added and the whole

52
00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:13.210
post-production process can do what its actual job is, and that is to sweeten the

53
00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:17.689
final image so that it looks better and it doesn't have to worry about making story

54
00:04:17.689 --> 00:04:18.689
corrections.

55
00:04:18.689 --> 00:04:22.970
And this was the big weakness of the prior earlier model.

56
00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:28.490
A lot of producers and directors would have to try and fix a lot of their issues and

57
00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:34.329
problems in post-production, causing a number of various issues, everything ranging from

58
00:04:34.329 --> 00:04:39.089
having to potentially do a number of reshoots through a number of financial factors and

59
00:04:39.089 --> 00:04:42.889
cost inflation, just it creates issues.

60
00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:47.769
And then ultimately the film is completed and, of course, heads out through distribution.

61
00:04:48.769 --> 00:04:53.930
If we look at it from another perspective, this is just another graph that kind of

62
00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:55.529
describes the same process.

63
00:04:55.529 --> 00:04:57.170
But let's just look at it for a second.

64
00:04:57.529 --> 00:05:02.889
You have the whole idea creation and it heads into this whole vortex of creative

65
00:05:02.889 --> 00:05:06.490
elements. You have the art department, you have the VFX department, the previs

66
00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:08.810
department, and the virtual art department.

67
00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:13.329
They're generating all of this content that is going to be moved over into Unreal

68
00:05:13.329 --> 00:05:19.009
Engine and you create assets that can be utilized throughout the rest of the entire

69
00:05:19.009 --> 00:05:20.850
pipeline. This is critical.

70
00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:27.209
Once these assets are available and ready within Unreal Engine, they can inform

71
00:05:27.209 --> 00:05:34.449
things like your stunt vis, more on your previs and any kind of technical vis that

72
00:05:34.449 --> 00:05:38.529
you need to construct to figure out specific issues and problems.

73
00:05:39.279 --> 00:05:43.639
Finally, when it moves into principal photography, you have all these different

74
00:05:43.639 --> 00:05:48.360
elements working in concert that are being fed by these assets that are being

75
00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:53.920
developed. So whether you're shooting something on green screen or you're shooting

76
00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.680
it live action, the virtual art department, all of it works together within principal

77
00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:05.839
photography to produce a final image that can go out to editorial into post and

78
00:06:05.839 --> 00:06:08.279
finally get your final edit.

79
00:06:09.199 --> 00:06:16.079
Now, we noticed that there are a number of key departments that we're familiar with in

80
00:06:16.079 --> 00:06:17.680
classical film production.

81
00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.240
You have those that are above the line and those that are below the line.

82
00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:25.839
Above the line, you know, director, producer, executive producer, cast and

83
00:06:25.839 --> 00:06:27.800
casting director, classic.

84
00:06:28.959 --> 00:06:34.439
But below the line, you have a number of different departments and there are some

85
00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:40.000
distinct departments that are very much affected by the ICV effects process.

86
00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:41.120
Let's take a look at them.

87
00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:46.220
They are production, art, camera and VFX.

88
00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:52.129
Of all the departments that are impacted by ICV effects, it's these four that are

89
00:06:52.129 --> 00:06:53.850
probably impacted the most.

90
00:06:55.009 --> 00:07:01.850
ICV effects also has a tendency to require the use of various sub departments.

91
00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:07.649
Now, these can be sub departments that can be provided by third party vendors or they

92
00:07:07.649 --> 00:07:16.730
may be actually, you know, some ICV effects specific departments within the company

93
00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:20.089
that's providing your LED wall.

94
00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:23.850
But if we look at them, we've already identified what the VAD is.

95
00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:28.529
This is the virtual art department, handles asset ingestion, optimization and

96
00:07:28.529 --> 00:07:29.689
environment creation.

97
00:07:30.209 --> 00:07:35.730
But then you have a need for additional sub departments like animation.

98
00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:41.129
You might need to have some specific artists on board to create various

99
00:07:41.129 --> 00:07:44.889
animated elements for anything that you might need on stage.

100
00:07:45.209 --> 00:07:47.730
So having a few animators on hand is important.

101
00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:50.170
You have CG lighting.

102
00:07:50.569 --> 00:07:51.689
That is a factor.

103
00:07:52.089 --> 00:07:58.209
So these individuals will help you with your light planning for the LED volume, tone

104
00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:02.449
and per shot lighting during principal photography and various post lighting.

105
00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:07.089
So, you know, when you're dealing with something like an LED wall, you're not only

106
00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:11.930
utilizing the lights that you have practically on stage, but also the light

107
00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:14.050
that's coming off of the LED wall itself.

108
00:08:14.449 --> 00:08:16.850
You need some mocap specialists for sure.

109
00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:22.089
This is basically to help you handle performance capture, including face and

110
00:08:22.089 --> 00:08:28.089
fingers, if you need that for any kind of animated element, along with handling

111
00:08:28.329 --> 00:08:29.930
the tracking of the camera itself.

112
00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:32.690
That's also handled through motion capture.

113
00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:35.649
There is the need for camera specialists.

114
00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:40.649
This is basically to help you with location scouting, more previous and

115
00:08:40.649 --> 00:08:47.850
layout camera consulting and, you know, setting up your environments and worlds.

116
00:08:47.889 --> 00:08:53.370
So these cameras will help you figure out what are the best angles to

117
00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:54.730
capture this information.

118
00:08:55.169 --> 00:09:00.129
And if necessary, you can work with your artists to potentially move various

119
00:09:00.129 --> 00:09:04.889
elements in the digital world as a form of location scouting and modification.

120
00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:09.809
Finally, you're probably going to need some sort of editorial team that's going

121
00:09:09.809 --> 00:09:15.529
to be available to assemble rough cuts of all of this information throughout

122
00:09:15.529 --> 00:09:16.690
the entire process.

123
00:09:17.679 --> 00:09:18.159
All right.

124
00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.840
Now for roles and responsibilities, there are several.

125
00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:26.080
We have a number of different roles that we'll identify.

126
00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:29.720
Basically, you are pretty much familiar with the director.

127
00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.879
He's the guy who supplies the creative vision and the direction for the film.

128
00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:39.559
But you also have other big major key players, like the virtual production

129
00:09:39.559 --> 00:09:41.600
supervisor and his team of artists.

130
00:09:41.919 --> 00:09:45.960
So basically the virtual production supervisor is going to be taken care of

131
00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:49.039
all those things that involve the wall and the volume.

132
00:09:50.039 --> 00:09:55.240
He and his artists, or she and his artists, they're kind of similar to people

133
00:09:55.240 --> 00:10:00.000
who are skilled in the practical world around camera, those elements within

134
00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:04.000
some things with cinematography, but it's all from the virtual side of the

135
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:04.440
house.

136
00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:09.159
Next, you need to have a virtual production colorist.

137
00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:13.960
This is basically to ensure that the LED color science is accurate.

138
00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:18.440
This is extremely critical because what you're shooting through camera and what

139
00:10:18.519 --> 00:10:21.600
you're displaying on the wall, you need to make sure that all of your color

140
00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:22.799
science is in alignment.

141
00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.360
Otherwise you might not have a very accurate color matching.

142
00:10:28.039 --> 00:10:29.399
You have a stage manager.

143
00:10:29.879 --> 00:10:34.799
This is the person that is basically handling all stage operations and what's

144
00:10:34.799 --> 00:10:36.200
happening within the volume.

145
00:10:36.919 --> 00:10:40.679
You have your VAD supervisor and all of the artists within the virtual art

146
00:10:40.679 --> 00:10:41.240
department.

147
00:10:41.279 --> 00:10:45.039
They're handling the design and the construction of all the various virtual

148
00:10:45.039 --> 00:10:48.159
assets that are going to be placed up on the wall itself.

149
00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:51.799
You have the MoCap supervisor and various operators.

150
00:10:51.799 --> 00:10:56.519
These are the folks that are basically handling all the tracking and performance

151
00:10:56.519 --> 00:10:58.919
management of anything that's being captured.

152
00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:01.909
You have pipeline developers and engineers.

153
00:11:01.909 --> 00:11:06.269
Now this is an unusual set of people and roles that you don't commonly find in

154
00:11:06.269 --> 00:11:07.750
traditional production.

155
00:11:08.029 --> 00:11:13.470
By having engineers and pipeline developers available, they can customize the Unreal

156
00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:17.269
engine and add specific code or data that you might want to integrate.

157
00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:19.549
And they're a great benefit to have around.

158
00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:24.950
And then finally, of course, your animation FX, TD, and rigging personnel.

159
00:11:25.309 --> 00:11:28.470
Any of these folks are basically going to be helping with the animation

160
00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:32.149
process and they're also critical.

161
00:11:33.419 --> 00:11:37.059
Next, I'd like to get into the various benefits of virtual production as a whole.

162
00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:40.220
Like I said, virtual production is this umbrella term.

163
00:11:40.259 --> 00:11:44.019
So it is something that we should think about in that sense.

164
00:11:44.059 --> 00:11:49.379
Number one, there's the whole idea of like a unified asset creation pipeline.

165
00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:53.419
And when we think about that, I mentioned earlier that when working in

166
00:11:53.419 --> 00:11:58.539
Unreal engine, the whole goal is basically being able to create assets

167
00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:03.059
early in pre-production that can be utilized during production and pushed

168
00:12:03.059 --> 00:12:03.820
into post.

169
00:12:04.059 --> 00:12:10.820
When that happens, you have a possibility of saving funds and saving time with not

170
00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:15.179
needing to recreate the wheel or create new assets for new phases of the

171
00:12:15.179 --> 00:12:16.259
production process.

172
00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:20.620
Next, it's a programmable real-time production tool set.

173
00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:25.620
I mentioned this earlier, the ability to alter or modify things in the code

174
00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:30.139
of Unreal engine is really important and they can help you create custom

175
00:12:30.139 --> 00:12:33.779
tools that you wouldn't normally find on the day.

176
00:12:34.669 --> 00:12:37.909
Next, remote and multi-user capabilities.

177
00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:43.750
This is a unique prospect here in that you can have individuals assisting and

178
00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:48.950
contributing to the shooting process on stage who may not actually be located at

179
00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:49.870
the stage itself.

180
00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:55.629
Multi-user capability can allow for people to remote into this process, be able

181
00:12:55.629 --> 00:13:03.830
to potentially provide feedback and or provide other types of graphics and

182
00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:06.909
performance via motion capture or anything really.

183
00:13:07.389 --> 00:13:13.669
But the whole multi-user capabilities within engine not only extends out to the

184
00:13:13.669 --> 00:13:18.149
people outside of the stage, but also the number of different stations and

185
00:13:18.149 --> 00:13:21.230
computers that are set up on the stage so that they can be all

186
00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:25.590
interconnected and talking to one another, sharing those graphics and 3D

187
00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:27.629
assets amongst themselves.

188
00:13:28.309 --> 00:13:31.990
There's the aspect of allowing for real-time physics and simulations.

189
00:13:32.029 --> 00:13:38.350
So again, if you want to have a potential animation that includes physics or, you

190
00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:43.710
know, various simulations like cloth simulations or wind or various

191
00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:47.429
environmental effects, that's something that can be done as well.

192
00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:51.590
There's a reduced requirement on the rendering process for all this.

193
00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:56.070
We'll get into the hardware specifically about what's needed to render in real

194
00:13:56.070 --> 00:14:01.789
time, but instead of having to have a large render farm in order to see some

195
00:14:01.789 --> 00:14:03.830
of this, this is going to all be rendered in real time.

196
00:14:03.830 --> 00:14:05.149
And you have that interaction.

197
00:14:05.669 --> 00:14:10.269
Real-time camera tracking, we mentioned this about the mocap personnel, but you

198
00:14:10.269 --> 00:14:13.909
know, the cameras is tracked a hundred percent of the time.

199
00:14:13.909 --> 00:14:18.269
So wherever the camera moves in real life, you'll see it update on the stage.

200
00:14:18.909 --> 00:14:23.309
And finally, the whole data management aspect of all of this is tracked

201
00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:26.509
through various source control applications with inside of the

202
00:14:26.509 --> 00:14:30.909
engine, combining it with other third-party tool sets like Perforce,

203
00:14:31.269 --> 00:14:33.590
which can keep track of all of your data.

204
00:14:34.269 --> 00:14:40.350
Now getting a little more specific, there are some specific benefits to ICV effects.

205
00:14:40.429 --> 00:14:46.190
Now this is again, not virtual production as a whole, but ICV effects specifically.

206
00:14:46.429 --> 00:14:49.509
Number one in camera finals, this is huge.

207
00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:53.909
You know, the ability to basically be able to shoot something through the

208
00:14:53.909 --> 00:14:59.909
camera and have all of your elements in stage and get a final output or

209
00:14:59.909 --> 00:15:02.789
a final shot, that's pretty important.

210
00:15:03.269 --> 00:15:06.549
There's the whole aspect of proper reflections and lighting.

211
00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:09.669
Big thing for being on an LED volume.

212
00:15:09.710 --> 00:15:15.549
If you have all these LEDs behind you, delivering you some type of environment,

213
00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:19.710
anything that's reflective inside of the volume, like perhaps a person's

214
00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:25.830
glasses or anything like a car or some sort of glass, this can pick up these

215
00:15:25.830 --> 00:15:30.190
reflections that are on the LEDs and give you an even greater sense of

216
00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:35.830
realism that you wouldn't be able to do, you know, using prior techniques.

217
00:15:35.870 --> 00:15:40.230
You would have to probably comp, you know, reflections into glass or

218
00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:43.350
something like that, using a more traditional approach.

219
00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:47.230
The LED volume can support dynamic animated backgrounds.

220
00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:51.909
So if you've got some animation in the background, perhaps flying birds or,

221
00:15:52.309 --> 00:15:57.230
you know, the process of smoke or, you know, swaying trees, all of that

222
00:15:57.230 --> 00:16:02.429
can be animated and seen in the volume and captured live in camera.

223
00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:06.070
There's better coordination with the VFX team.

224
00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:10.549
When I showed you the workflow and pipeline, we have the VFX team now

225
00:16:10.549 --> 00:16:12.549
keenly interested in this process.

226
00:16:12.870 --> 00:16:18.110
So there, they will be more readily available in order to discuss these

227
00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:21.350
options about how to make this whole process work smoothly.

228
00:16:22.029 --> 00:16:24.029
There's rapid lighting and changeovers.

229
00:16:24.149 --> 00:16:27.350
This is a big advantage of using the wall as well.

230
00:16:27.549 --> 00:16:30.990
You can simply change out your environment and have a completely different

231
00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:32.950
lighting setup at any point in time.

232
00:16:33.629 --> 00:16:35.750
There's the flexibility to improvise on set.

233
00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:39.669
You can move your set pieces around along with moving your digital set

234
00:16:39.669 --> 00:16:43.269
pieces around to get a completely different composition.

235
00:16:44.059 --> 00:16:45.860
Reduction on practical sets.

236
00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:48.299
Again, self-explanatory.

237
00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:51.779
If a lot of the background sets can be constructed digitally, there'll

238
00:16:51.779 --> 00:16:55.539
be no need to actually try to construct that practically.

239
00:16:55.539 --> 00:16:57.100
So it's a big benefit there.

240
00:16:57.659 --> 00:17:02.779
There's the continuity of vision through post, meaning anything that you have

241
00:17:02.779 --> 00:17:06.619
generated for this LED wall can be utilized in post-production as well

242
00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:08.660
as part of the assets that are used.

243
00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:12.539
So things that are rendered for the wall can also be used

244
00:17:12.539 --> 00:17:14.539
as elements in final post.

245
00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:19.059
All of this is all captured and maintained digitally.

246
00:17:19.059 --> 00:17:23.420
So, Hey, if you need to do a pickup or a reshoot of some particular shot,

247
00:17:23.779 --> 00:17:27.740
you can just bring that environment back up on the wall and you're back

248
00:17:27.740 --> 00:17:32.779
in business in fairly short order, as opposed to having to go back on location,

249
00:17:33.099 --> 00:17:39.740
potentially setting up new content in order to capture those shots.

250
00:17:40.059 --> 00:17:42.339
Plus there's the whole issue of time and day.

251
00:17:42.579 --> 00:17:46.099
When we're working on a wall, you can stay at golden hour

252
00:17:46.099 --> 00:17:47.220
for however long you need.

253
00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:50.579
There's a whole aspect of rich mid-tones and color values.

254
00:17:50.779 --> 00:17:53.779
The wall just gives a really nice diffusion of light.

255
00:17:53.779 --> 00:17:56.859
It's really quite beautiful, but that also can be a limitation,

256
00:17:56.859 --> 00:17:58.180
which we'll get into in a moment.

257
00:17:58.859 --> 00:18:01.180
There's the whole aspect of visual parallax.

258
00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:06.140
As you move the camera, you get 3D parallax on the screen behind our actors.

259
00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:11.500
And, you know, there's also the possibility of shooting all types

260
00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:12.859
of different camera angles.

261
00:18:13.299 --> 00:18:16.019
And I mentioned the whole aspect of diffused lighting.

262
00:18:17.109 --> 00:18:20.230
All right, let's talk about some of the limitations of ICD effects,

263
00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:24.670
because as good as the volume is, it can't handle doing everything.

264
00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:28.710
So first on the list, we have transitions from the practical

265
00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:30.549
to virtual world can be problematic.

266
00:18:30.869 --> 00:18:31.950
What do I mean by this?

267
00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:34.950
Well, you only have so much space within the volume.

268
00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:39.390
You've got, you know, it could be anywhere from 10 feet

269
00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:42.309
to maybe 30 or 40 feet, whatever it may be.

270
00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:47.190
If you want to move objects from the practical world into the background,

271
00:18:47.309 --> 00:18:49.710
this can be something that has to be thought about

272
00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:52.430
with a certain amount of care.

273
00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:57.230
Or if you're having digital elements somehow move on to the practical stage,

274
00:18:57.869 --> 00:19:01.509
you're going to have to find a way to transition this to be more seamless.

275
00:19:01.509 --> 00:19:03.430
And that can be problematic.

276
00:19:03.869 --> 00:19:06.430
Next, we have mutual assets

277
00:19:07.029 --> 00:19:10.349
usually are required for both virtual and practical sets.

278
00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:13.390
Now, this is quite important.

279
00:19:13.910 --> 00:19:17.390
Normally, when we talk about items that are on the stage,

280
00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:19.910
you have items that you come to the stage with.

281
00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.990
Maybe these are practical set pieces that you've built

282
00:19:23.789 --> 00:19:26.509
or maybe it's digital assets that you have in CG.

283
00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:29.309
You need to be able to have a certain amount of well,

284
00:19:29.309 --> 00:19:33.069
you have to have exact parity between these two elements.

285
00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:37.549
So if you started off and you have a CG element

286
00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:40.990
that needs to be seen in the practical world,

287
00:19:41.069 --> 00:19:45.869
you're going to have to build that practical chair or set piece or table.

288
00:19:46.349 --> 00:19:50.869
Or if you've come to the stage with something practically

289
00:19:50.869 --> 00:19:52.990
and you need to see it in the background,

290
00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:56.309
you're also going to then have to build that in CG

291
00:19:56.309 --> 00:19:59.549
in order for it to be seen in the ICD effects volume.

292
00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:03.980
Next, there's the whole aspect of animated lighting in the virtual world

293
00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:07.220
can be difficult to match practically. Very true.

294
00:20:07.779 --> 00:20:10.099
Though this is getting a lot better,

295
00:20:10.099 --> 00:20:13.059
we want to make sure that the color science is always accurate.

296
00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:16.619
And this leads to issues about the LED black point,

297
00:20:17.019 --> 00:20:20.579
various color science issues, practical light contamination.

298
00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:24.099
And that can be a big headache for a lot of people, especially

299
00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:29.579
if you have lights that are shining more direct light into the stage.

300
00:20:29.579 --> 00:20:33.539
There's the chance that you might get spill onto the LED panels themselves,

301
00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:37.220
which can potentially desaturate a lot of the images

302
00:20:37.220 --> 00:20:40.140
on the panels that you're seeing.

303
00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:43.940
It can be potentially detected through the camera when you're shooting.

304
00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:49.500
This light contamination can be, like I said, very problematic.

305
00:20:50.259 --> 00:20:54.220
Next, there's the issue of hard shadows and mimicking daylight.

306
00:20:54.339 --> 00:20:55.940
This can be very difficult as well.

307
00:20:55.940 --> 00:21:01.140
Very hard shadows is not something that comes easy on an LED volume.

308
00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:05.740
The LED lights themselves, they produce a really beautiful diffuse light,

309
00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:09.140
but they don't do much for actually producing hard shadows.

310
00:21:09.579 --> 00:21:12.539
So in order to do that, you're going to have to introduce,

311
00:21:12.779 --> 00:21:16.779
you know, these stronger practical lights that can be shined in on stage

312
00:21:16.779 --> 00:21:18.539
in order to cast these hard shadows.

313
00:21:18.539 --> 00:21:20.619
And that's where you have to be careful.

314
00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:24.259
Next, reflective surfaces in the virtual world.

315
00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:25.859
This can be an issue.

316
00:21:25.859 --> 00:21:29.380
So, you know, you don't want to include anything in your virtual world

317
00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:32.500
that might be a mirror or, you know, highly reflective

318
00:21:32.819 --> 00:21:36.420
so that when an actor walks past that in the virtual world,

319
00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:39.380
you're not going to be able to see the reflection of that actor

320
00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:42.859
in that mirror or on that chrome surface or something like that.

321
00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:44.980
So just keep that in mind.

322
00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:47.019
There are some limitations on the frame rate.

323
00:21:47.339 --> 00:21:50.700
Right now, for most stages that are out there now,

324
00:21:50.859 --> 00:21:55.539
they probably have a maximum frame rate of 48 frames per second.

325
00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:58.460
Most things are typically shot in 24 frames per second.

326
00:21:58.779 --> 00:22:01.259
There are some that can do it in 48 frames per second.

327
00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:05.420
But if you're wanting to shoot really high speed slow-mo,

328
00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:09.779
you know, something like 120 frames or 240 frames per second,

329
00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:15.460
that's something that is coming, but isn't quite available yet.

330
00:22:16.099 --> 00:22:20.700
Next, this whole process, though it is rendered in real time

331
00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:23.740
and on a number of great graphics cards,

332
00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:26.180
it still requires a lot of compute resources.

333
00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:30.299
You are going to need a machine room to house a lot of the computers

334
00:22:30.299 --> 00:22:32.299
that are doing a lot of the work,

335
00:22:32.779 --> 00:22:36.779
because it's not just the machine that's rendering Unreal Engine.

336
00:22:36.779 --> 00:22:39.460
There are a whole series of additional pieces of equipment.

337
00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:42.539
And this, of course, requires a lot of resources

338
00:22:42.539 --> 00:22:45.500
to maintain power, HVAC, and so forth.

339
00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:50.539
Next, the FX elements, these can be difficult to integrate too.

340
00:22:50.539 --> 00:22:52.140
And these are the effects elements

341
00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:55.380
that you might want to put into Unreal Engine,

342
00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:57.420
like explosions in the background,

343
00:22:57.420 --> 00:23:00.460
or some sort of particle system or lighting system.

344
00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:05.099
You're going to have to potentially deal with that

345
00:23:05.099 --> 00:23:08.259
in your live action element as well.

346
00:23:08.259 --> 00:23:10.220
So how do those lights interact?

347
00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:12.299
How do you make everything work?
